---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ConfidenceBadge from '../components/ConfidenceBadge.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About"
  description="Who makes Qart-Hadasht and why: a sourced, positions-taking encyclopedia of ancient Carthage that gives the city a better place on the open web, with disclosed methods and an open invitation to corrections."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 prose-encyclopedia">
    <h1 class="font-serif mb-3">About this project</h1>
    <p class="text-sand-700 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
      Qart-Hadasht is an evolving encyclopedia of ancient Carthage, from
      its founding to its destruction in 146 BCE. Built as a structured,
      cited reference where every claim, source, and gap is named.
    </p>

    <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
    <p>
      I'm not a historian. I'm just someone who loves ancient Carthage and
      thinks the city deserves a better place on the open web than it
      currently has. Most of what survives about Carthage was written by its
      enemies; what little is now widely read is mostly the dramatic
      highlights (Hannibal crossing the Alps, the destruction in 146 BCE)
      with the long arc and the structural questions left out. I wanted a
      reference I could actually use, and decided to build one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Practically: every page on this site cites its sources, distinguishes
      what's attested from what's inferred or contested, and is open to
      correction. The synthesis is mine; the underlying scholarship is
      everyone else's, and is named at the bottom of each claim and source
      page. Where I take a position (the labeled "editorial takes"), I show
      my reasoning. Where I'm wrong, please tell me.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is built with a content-modeled approach (events, people,
      places, sources, claims, themes, institutions) that lets me cross-link
      everything bidirectionally and keeps the underlying data structured
      enough that academics could in principle query it. Whether that's
      useful to anyone other than me is a question the site hopes to answer.
    </p>

    <!-- Contact form. Netlify Forms picks this up at build time via the
         data-netlify attribute and the hidden form-name field. Honeypot
         field "bot-field" is hidden visually but visible to bots; Netlify
         drops submissions that fill it in. -->
    <aside class="not-prose mt-8 mb-10 p-5 border border-sand-300 rounded-lg bg-sand-50">
      <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-tyrian-700">Get in touch</p>
      <p class="font-serif text-xl text-ink-900 mt-1">Found something wrong, or want to share a source?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-sand-900 mt-2 mb-5">
        Drop a note below. Corrections, source recommendations, and good-faith
        disagreements all welcome.
      </p>

      <form
        name="contact"
        method="POST"
        data-netlify="true"
        netlify-honeypot="bot-field"
        action="/thanks"
        class="space-y-4"
      >
        <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="contact" />

        <!-- Honeypot: hidden from real users, visible to spam bots. -->
        <p class="hidden" aria-hidden="true">
          <label>
            Don't fill this out if you're human:
            <input name="bot-field" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
          </label>
        </p>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div>
            <label for="contact-name" class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold text-sand-700 mb-1">Name</label>
            <input
              type="text"
              id="contact-name"
              name="name"
              autocomplete="name"
              class="w-full px-3 py-2 border border-sand-300 rounded bg-white text-sm text-ink-900 focus:outline-none focus:border-tyrian-500"
            />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="contact-title" class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold text-sand-700 mb-1">Title</label>
            <input
              type="text"
              id="contact-title"
              name="title"
              placeholder="e.g. Professor of Classics, student, amateur historian"
              class="w-full px-3 py-2 border border-sand-300 rounded bg-white text-sm text-ink-900 placeholder:text-sand-500 focus:outline-none focus:border-tyrian-500"
            />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="contact-email" class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold text-sand-700 mb-1">Email</label>
          <input
            type="email"
            id="contact-email"
            name="email"
            autocomplete="email"
            placeholder="optional, only if you'd like a reply"
            class="w-full px-3 py-2 border border-sand-300 rounded bg-white text-sm text-ink-900 placeholder:text-sand-500 focus:outline-none focus:border-tyrian-500"
          />
        </div>

        <div>
          <label for="contact-message" class="block text-xs uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold text-sand-700 mb-1">Message</label>
          <textarea
            id="contact-message"
            name="message"
            rows="6"
            required
            class="w-full px-3 py-2 border border-sand-300 rounded bg-white text-sm text-ink-900 focus:outline-none focus:border-tyrian-500 font-sans"
          ></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="flex justify-end">
          <button
            type="submit"
            class="px-5 py-2 bg-tyrian-700 text-sand-50 text-sm font-semibold rounded hover:bg-tyrian-900 active:bg-tyrian-100 active:text-tyrian-900 transition-colors"
          >
            Send message
          </button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </aside>

    <h2>The name</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="font-phoenician text-tyrian-700">𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕</span> —
      <em>Qart-Hadasht</em>, "New City" — is what the Carthaginians called
      Carthage. "Carthage" itself is the Latin form, by way of the Greek
      <em>Karchedon</em>. Most of what survives about Carthage was written by
      its enemies; using the city's own name, on its own terms, is part of how
      this project tries to push past that.
    </p>

    <h2>How this site works</h2>
    <p>
      Every factual statement is modeled as a <strong>claim</strong>: atomic,
      individually citable, and tagged with a confidence label
      (<ConfidenceBadge confidence="attested" size="xs" />,
      <ConfidenceBadge confidence="inferred" size="xs" />,
      <ConfidenceBadge confidence="contested" size="xs" />, or
      <ConfidenceBadge confidence="legendary" size="xs" />). Each claim
      links to the ancient and modern sources that support, qualify, or
      contradict it. Source pages, in turn, are digests of what each
      source preserves about Carthaginian history, with metadata on
      reliability: author, language, date of composition, distance from
      events, known biases.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the evidence supports a synthesis, the site occasionally offers
      a labeled <strong>editorial take</strong> with its reasoning and the
      competing positions named. Where we genuinely don't know, the gap
      itself gets a page as an open question.
    </p>
    <aside class="not-prose mt-6 mb-8 p-5 border border-tyrian-300 bg-sand-50 rounded-lg">
      <p class="text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest font-bold text-tyrian-700">Methodology</p>
      <p class="font-serif text-lg text-tyrian-900 mt-1">How we know what we know</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-sand-900 mt-2">
        The full methodology page covers the confidence vocabulary, source
        distance and access, the supports/qualifies/contradicts stance
        glyphs, editorial-take confidence, and how narratives, themes, and
        open questions fit alongside the claim layer.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3"><a href="/methodology" class="text-tyrian-700 font-medium hover:underline">Read the methodology →</a></p>
    </aside>

    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
      The principal focus runs from the legendary founding (traditionally
      814 BCE) to the destruction of Carthage in 146 BCE. Roman Carthage as
      its own polity, and Carthage's long afterlife in literature and modern
      memory, sit outside the principal Punic-period focus.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the site rejects the "Carthage was destroyed and that was the end"
      framing as residual Greco-Roman closure narrative. Punic cultural,
      linguistic, and religious identity persisted long after 146 BCE — in
      neo-Punic inscriptions through at least the 3rd century CE, in the
      Tanit-Iuno Caelestis and Baal-Saturnus cults through at least the 4th,
      in Augustine's testimony to spoken Punic in his early-5th-century North
      African congregations, and in Mago of Carthage's agricultural treatise
      transmitted continuously through Roman, Greek, Arabic, and medieval
      Latin agronomic literature into the European Renaissance. Where this
      continuity is load-bearing for understanding the Punic civilization's
      substantive arc, the site treats it directly — see the
      <a href="/narratives/punic-continuity-in-roman-africa">continuity in Roman Africa narrative</a>
      and the open question on the
      <a href="/openQuestions/duration-of-punic-cultural-identity">duration of Punic cultural identity</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensing</h2>
    <p>
      Content is licensed CC&nbsp;BY&nbsp;4.0: quote it, cite it, build on it
      freely with attribution. Code is MIT.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
